%N A400835 After the initial 0, numbers k such that k is a multiple of A007947(A327860(k)).
%C A400835 Each term is found either in A276156 or in A381037.
%C A400835 Numbers k that satisfy A007947(A327860(k)) = A007947(k), 0, 1, 7, 8, 36, 91, 1056, 2556, (and apparently no others?), is a subsequence of this sequence.
%C A400835 13690208256 < a(69) <= A002110(11) = 200560490130.
%H A400835 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A400835/b400835.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..68</a>
%H A400835 <a href="/index/Pri#primorialbase">Index entries for sequences related to primorial base</a>.
%F A400835 {k such that either k = 0 or k == 0 mod A007947(A327860(k))}.
%o A400835 (PARI) is_A400835(n) = if(0==n, 1, my(orgn=n,s=0, m=1, p=2, e); while(n, e = (n%p); if(e>1 && (orgn%p), return(0)); m *= (p^e); s += (e/p); n = n\p; p = nextprime(1+p)); !(orgn%(factorback(factorint(s*m)[, 1]))));
%Y A400835 Cf. A007947, A327860.
%Y A400835 Subsequence of (A276156 U A381037).
